<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="sub-legendre-adrien-marie" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Adrien-Marie Legendre</title>
  <idx>Adrien-Marie Legendre</idx>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        <alert>Years:</alert> 1752-1833
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Citizenship:</alert> Italian-French
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Core Contributions:</alert>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Developed the <xref ref="def-legendre-transform" text="custom">Legendre transform</xref> which converts
              convex functions into functions of their derivative variable.
            </p>
            <p>
              The <xref ref="def-legendre-transform" text="custom">Legendre transform</xref> was later shown that it
              could be used as a computational tool to convert a system of <m>n</m>
              <xref ref="def-euler-lagrange" text="custom">Euler-Lagrange Equations</xref> into a system of <m>2n</m>
              Hamiltonian equations.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Formulated Legendre's differential equation.
            </p>
            <p>
              Shows up in expansions for:
              <ul>
                <li>
                  Gravitational waves.
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Electric potentials when there is spherical symmetry.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    This bridges <xref ref="gls-spherical-harmonics" text="custom">spherical harmonics</xref> and the
                    wavefunction for the Hydrogen atom.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ul>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </introduction>

</subsection>